Key Takeaways from QA Feedback
1. Requirement Discussions
 - Share early context and walk QA through the problem you are solving.
 - Define scope, user scenarios, and acceptance criteria clearly to avoid assumptions.
 - Call out dependencies and open items so QA can plan coverage early.

2. Story Writing
 - Write user stories with detailed acceptance criteria and sample data.
 - Add business rules, validation checks, and edge cases (permissions, data size, etc.).
 - This helps QA validate faster and prevents missed scenarios.

3. Edge Case Collaboration
 - Discuss “what-if” cases before development begins.
 - Cover negative scenarios, existing user impact, and cross-module effects.
 - A short brainstorming session early avoids last-minute changes later.

4. Sign-Off and Release
 - Involve QA in demos or review calls before sign-off.
 - Set clear pass/fail expectations and acceptable deviations to  close stories faster.


